  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "Just as recurrent mutation to a deleterious allele can maintain the allele in a population in spite of selection against individuals that carry the allele, resulting in a mutation-selection balance (see Chapter 5), recurrent migration can maintain a deleterious allele in a state of migration-selection balance. This situation can arise when an allele is deleterious in one geographical region but not deleterious, or less deleterious, in a neighboring geographical region. Migrants from the latter region continually replenish the deleterious allele in the former region, where selection acts against it.",
      "source_page": 321,
      "source_pages": [
        321,
        322
      ],
      "source_block_ids": [
        "PopGen_chapter6:p051:b5",
        "PopGen_chapter6:p052:b1"
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
